1
00:03:51,428 --> 00:03:57,428
An intimate bioscopic experience with The Beatles.

2
00:07:42,857 --> 00:07:48,857
Let It Be is a documentary, music film directed by Michael Lindsay-Hogg.

3
00:11:34,285 --> 00:11:40,285
A documentary chronicling the Beatles' rehearsal sessions in January 1969 for their proposed
"back to basics" album, "Get Back," later re-envisioned and released as "Let It Be."

4
00:15:25,714 --> 00:15:31,714
Self - The Beatles (uncredited), played by John Lennon.

5
00:19:17,142 --> 00:19:23,142
Self - The Beatles (uncredited), played by Paul McCartney.

6
00:23:08,571 --> 00:23:14,571
Self - The Beatles (uncredited), played by George Harrison.

7
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:06,000
Self - The Beatles (uncredited), played by Ringo Starr.

8
00:30:51,428 --> 00:30:57,428
Self (uncredited), played by Billy Preston.

9
00:34:42,857 --> 00:34:48,857
Self (uncredited), played by George Martin.

10
00:38:34,285 --> 00:38:40,285
Let It Be is a 1970 British documentary film starring the
Beatles and directed by Michael Lindsay-Hogg.

11
00:42:25,714 --> 00:42:31,714
The film documents the group's rehearsing and recording songs in January 1969 for
what was to become their twelfth and final studio album Let It Be.

12
00:46:17,142 --> 00:46:23,142
The film ends with an unannounced rooftop concert by the
group, their last public performance together.

13
00:50:08,571 --> 00:50:14,571
The film was originally planned as a television documentary
that would accompany a concert broadcast.

14
00:54:00,000 --> 00:54:06,000
When plans for the concert were dropped, the project
became a feature film production.

15
00:57:51,428 --> 00:57:57,428
Although Let It Be does not dwell on the dissension within the Beatles at the time, it
provides some glimpses into the dynamics that would lead to their break-up.

16
01:01:42,857 --> 01:01:48,857
After the film's release, John Lennon, Paul McCartney, George Harrison and
Ringo Starr won an Academy Award for Best Original Song Score.

17
01:05:34,285 --> 01:05:40,285
Footage filmed for Let It Be was later restored and re-edited for
Peter Jackson's 2021 documentary The Beatles: Get Back.

18
01:09:25,714 --> 01:09:31,714
Let It Be had not been officially available on home video since the
1980s, although bootleg copies of the film still circulated.

19
01:13:17,142 --> 01:13:23,142
While attempts to release the film on DVD and Blu-ray have not come to fruition, a restored 4K version
of the film was made available to stream on Disney+ for the first time on 8 May 2024.

20
01:17:08,571 --> 01:17:14,571
The film explores themes of concert, studio, rock band.
